<ansi fg="black-bold">.:</ansi> <ansi fg="magenta">Help for </ansi><ansi fg="command">summon-hive-swarm</ansi> spell

The <ansi fg="command">Summon Hive Swarm</ansi> spell shatters a Hive Fragment into a permanent
swarm servant that fights alongside the caster. The ritual demands
intense concentration to execute but yields a tireless ally capable
of harrying and overwhelming individual enemies.

Of every companion in the game, the swarm asks the least of you to
keep. It is a good choice beside a heavier bond, or for a summoner
whose will is already largely spoken for.

<ansi fg="yellow">Usage: </ansi>

  <ansi fg="command">cast summon-hive-swarm</ansi>

<ansi fg="yellow">Details: </ansi>

  <ansi fg="yellow">Type:        </ansi> Help Single
  <ansi fg="yellow">School:      </ansi> Mental
  <ansi fg="yellow">Base Folds:  </ansi> Long channel
  <ansi fg="yellow">Cast Cost:   </ansi> Light, and paid only once
  <ansi fg="yellow">Held Cost:   </ansi> Slight, for as long as you keep it

<ansi fg="white-bold">Notes:</ansi>
  - Requires a Hive Fragment in your inventory to cast.
  - The swarm persists until destroyed or dismissed.
  - Summoning it costs conviction once. Keeping it sets conviction
    aside for as long as it stays with you, and that held share,
    not the casting price, is what limits how many you can field.
  - A mightier companion holds more of your will. This one is
    among the least demanding you can bind.
  - Skill in manifestation lowers what a companion holds, so
    practice buys you a larger following as well as a stronger one.
  - A summoned swarm is the same wherever you stand. No remains
    are needed and none are consumed.
  - If your conviction is already spoken for, the summoning is
    refused outright rather than leaving you weaker, and costs
    you nothing.

